1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,824
Chai Corner now has five
outlets and thirty people.

2
00:00:04,124 --> 00:00:06,791
And all thirty still
report straight to Ravi.

3
00:00:07,341 --> 00:00:09,824
A new helper waits two days for an answer.

4
00:00:10,124 --> 00:00:13,632
A cash mistake at the bus
stand goes unseen for a week.

5
00:00:13,932 --> 00:00:17,154
Ravi works late every night,
and still falls behind.

6
00:00:17,804 --> 00:00:22,064
At the last festival rush, Ravi
tried to direct everyone himself.

7
00:00:22,414 --> 00:00:23,314
Tea here.

8
00:00:23,205 --> 00:00:24,105
Cash there.

9
00:00:24,092 --> 00:00:25,278
Watch the queue.

10
00:00:25,278 --> 00:00:27,748
One man, pointing in every direction.

11
00:00:28,098 --> 00:00:30,315
So he calls his core team together.

12
00:00:30,315 --> 00:00:31,864
Something has to change.

13
00:00:32,514 --> 00:00:34,260
The problem has a name.

14
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:35,523
Span of control.

15
00:00:35,823 --> 00:00:40,691
Span of control, or span of management,
is the number of people one manager

16
00:00:40,691 --> 00:00:42,079
can supervise well.

17
00:00:42,429 --> 00:00:44,799
Right now, Ravi's span is thirty.

18
00:00:44,799 --> 00:00:45,908
Far too wide.

19
00:00:46,558 --> 00:00:48,631
Why is thirty too many?

20
00:00:48,631 --> 00:00:51,815
In 1933, a management consultant, V.

21
00:00:51,815 --> 00:00:52,715
A.

22
00:00:52,315 --> 00:00:54,559
Graicunas, did the maths.

23
00:00:54,909 --> 00:00:59,125
A manager handles each person, every
small group, and the links between

24
00:00:59,125 --> 00:01:00,025
people.

25
00:01:00,090 --> 00:01:02,824
With two people, that
is six relationships.

26
00:01:02,824 --> 00:01:04,836
With five people, one hundred.

27
00:01:05,186 --> 00:01:08,294
With six, it jumps to two
hundred and twenty two.

28
00:01:08,294 --> 00:01:11,302
One more person, and the
load more than doubles.

29
00:01:11,952 --> 00:01:16,230
So Ravi gives every outlet a
manager, like Meena at the bus stand.

30
00:01:16,580 --> 00:01:21,341
Now Ravi guides five outlet managers,
and each one guides five people.

31
00:01:21,691 --> 00:01:23,137
Same thirty people.

32
00:01:23,137 --> 00:01:25,842
But now everyone has a boss close by.

33
00:01:26,492 --> 00:01:28,207
Now try a narrow span.

34
00:01:28,207 --> 00:01:30,711
Each manager guides just two people.

35
00:01:31,061 --> 00:01:34,061
That needs fifteen
managers, and five levels.

36
00:01:34,061 --> 00:01:35,590
The chart grows tall.

37
00:01:35,940 --> 00:01:37,354
Now a wide span.

38
00:01:37,354 --> 00:01:39,712
Each manager guides five people.

39
00:01:40,062 --> 00:01:42,511
Just six managers, and three levels.

40
00:01:42,511 --> 00:01:43,868
The chart is flat.

41
00:01:44,518 --> 00:01:46,323
Narrow span, tall chart.

42
00:01:46,323 --> 00:01:48,027
Wide span, flat chart.

43
00:01:48,727 --> 00:01:51,129
A tall chart gives close supervision.

44
00:01:51,129 --> 00:01:55,433
But messages crawl through every
level, and more managers cost more.

45
00:01:55,783 --> 00:01:58,705
A flat chart gives
freedom and fast messages.

46
00:01:58,705 --> 00:02:03,305
But each boss has less time for each
person, so staff must be skilled.

47
00:02:03,955 --> 00:02:06,655
So what sets the right span at work today?

48
00:02:06,655 --> 00:02:07,562
Five things.

49
00:02:07,912 --> 00:02:08,812
The work.

50
00:02:08,793 --> 00:02:11,822
Simple, repeated work allows a wide span.

51
00:02:11,822 --> 00:02:15,194
One supervisor can guide
a big team of packers.

52
00:02:15,544 --> 00:02:16,444
Skill.

53
00:02:16,130 --> 00:02:18,097
Skilled people need less help.

54
00:02:18,097 --> 00:02:21,148
A team of senior engineers
needs little checking.

55
00:02:21,498 --> 00:02:22,398
Guidance.

56
00:02:22,289 --> 00:02:23,838
New staff need more.

57
00:02:23,838 --> 00:02:27,052
A hospital pairs each
new nurse with a mentor.

58
00:02:27,402 --> 00:02:28,327
Technology.

59
00:02:28,327 --> 00:02:32,263
A team chat lets one manager
reach many people at once.

60
00:02:32,613 --> 00:02:33,513
Distance.

61
00:02:33,426 --> 00:02:36,070
Teams far apart are harder to watch.

62
00:02:36,070 --> 00:02:39,484
A regional manager has
to travel to each branch.

63
00:02:40,134 --> 00:02:42,635
Span also changes how work feels.

64
00:02:42,985 --> 00:02:45,902
Too narrow, and the
boss is always watching.

65
00:02:45,902 --> 00:02:48,078
People feel they are not trusted.

66
00:02:48,428 --> 00:02:50,654
Too wide, and nobody notices you.

67
00:02:50,654 --> 00:02:53,313
Problems wait, and the manager burns out.

68
00:02:53,663 --> 00:02:56,954
Chai work is simple, and
Ravi's managers are trained.

69
00:02:56,954 --> 00:02:58,802
So he keeps the flat chart.

70
00:02:59,152 --> 00:03:02,979
Now a new helper gets an answer
in one hour, not two days.

71
00:03:03,329 --> 00:03:04,229
The lesson.

72
00:03:04,138 --> 00:03:07,433
Wide enough to trust people,
narrow enough to help them.

73
00:03:08,083 --> 00:03:09,058
Quick check.

74
00:03:09,058 --> 00:03:12,041
Ravi wants faster messages
and fewer managers.

75
00:03:12,041 --> 00:03:13,689
Which should he choose?

76
00:03:15,589 --> 00:03:16,646
A wide span!

77
00:03:16,646 --> 00:03:20,589
Fewer levels, faster messages,
and fewer managers to pay.

78
00:03:21,189 --> 00:03:22,202
So, remember.

79
00:03:22,502 --> 00:03:26,960
Span of control is how many people
one manager can supervise well.

80
00:03:27,310 --> 00:03:29,499
A narrow span makes a tall chart.

81
00:03:29,499 --> 00:03:31,498
A wide span makes a flat one.

82
00:03:31,848 --> 00:03:36,608
The right span depends on the work,
skill, guidance, technology and distance.

83
00:03:37,208 --> 00:03:39,229
Next time, the series finale.

84
00:03:39,229 --> 00:03:41,537
What makes an organization effective?
